Token Bucket Algorithm:


    import java.time.Instant;

    public class TokenBucket {

        private final double refillRatePerSecond;
        private final double capacity;

        private double tokens;
        private long lastRefillTimestamp; // epoch millis

        public TokenBucket(double refillRatePerSecond, double capacity) {
            this.refillRatePerSecond = refillRatePerSecond;
            this.capacity = capacity;
            this.tokens = capacity;
            this.lastRefillTimestamp = Instant.now().toEpochMilli();
        }

        /*
            Here we use synchronized but this kind of setup will Works inside one JVM only.
            For distributed system we need to use Redis Lua. So, that all pods see same token bucket.
        */
        public synchronized boolean allowRequest() {
            refillTokens();

            if (tokens >= 1.0) {
                tokens -= 1.0;
                return true;
            }
            return false;
        }

        private void refillTokens() {
            long now = Instant.now().toEpochMilli();
            double secondsElapsed = (now - lastRefillTimestamp) / 1000.0;

            double tokensToAdd = secondsElapsed * refillRatePerSecond;
            tokens = Math.min(capacity, tokens + tokensToAdd);

            lastRefillTimestamp = now;
        }
    }

 2️⃣ Leaky Bucket Algorithm:
 📌 Intuition
        Requests enter a bucket and leak out at a fixed speed.
        Think of queue + constant drain.

⚙️ How it works:
    Requests go into a queue (bucket)
    Requests are processed at constant rate
    If bucket is full → reject new requests

✅ Advantages
    Smooth outgoing traffic
    Prevents traffic spikes completely
    Very predictable backend load

❌ Disadvantages
    No burst support
    Higher latency
    Not user-friendly for APIs

 🏭 Real-world usage:
    Network traffic shaping
    Video streaming

🎥 Why Leaky Bucket is PERFECT for Video Streaming

Problem in video streaming
    Users may request video chunks in bursts
    Backend (storage / CDN / transcoder) must receive traffic at a stable rate

Sudden spikes cause:
    Buffer underruns
    Encoder overload
    Network congestion
👉 We want smooth outgoing traffic, not bursty.
That’s exactly what Leaky Bucket guarantees.


🪣 Leaky Bucket – Real-World Meaning
    Incoming video chunk requests are queued
    Chunks are sent to the client at a fixed rate

Think:
    Bucket → queue of video chunks
    Leak rate → streaming bitrate (e.g., 5 MB/sec)

🔁 How it works in streaming
    Client requests chunks aggressively (seek / fast-forward*)
    Requests go into a bucket (queue)
    Server leaks chunks at constant speed

If bucket overflows → drop / reject new chunks

Token Bucket vs Leaky Bucket
    Unlike the Leaky Bucket, the Token Bucket allows the system to handle more request than the fixed
    rste for short period.

So, Token Bucket is flexible in handling short term burst request.

3. Fixed Window Algorithm
The fixed window algorithm categorizes(break) time into fixed intervals known as windows, and it
restricts the requests to specific numbers in the window(limit of acceptance of request in a window).
That is if the limit is exceeded, the requests are closed
down and they cannot be made again, at least until the next allowance or window.
In this counter reset to zero at start of every new window.

⚙️ How it works
    Time divided into windows (e.g., 1 minute)
    Count requests per window
    If count > limit → reject

🏭 Real-world usage:
    Login attempt limits
    Admin tools
    Non-critical APIs

Challenges:
    Can lead to bursts at the boundary of windows. Potentially upto twice the limit,
    Although very well suited for static traffic they are not suitable very much when it comes to
    variable traffic patterns.

Well suited where short term spike are allowed.


4️⃣ Sliding Window Algorithm:
 The sliding window algorithm is actually a variation of the fixed window algorithm.
 It keeps a moving time frame and restricts the number of requests to be made within that frame.
 It provides for a finer and basically a better rate limiting in that the window is most likely renewed
 and the rate ideally spread out evenly over a period such that there can be adequate control over the
 traffic direction and occurrence.

 📌 Intuition
    Fixed window, but smarter.
 Counts requests in a rolling time range.

 ⚙️ How it works
 Two approaches:
 A) Sliding Window Log (accurate)
     Store timestamp of every accepted request
     Remove old timestamps [check beginning of queue]
     Count remaining
     ✔ Accurate

